Seaside economics
1. The Economic Engine & Financial Flow
•	The Core Inflow (Tourism & TLT): Seaside’s financial ecosystem relies on an intricate balance between tourism export revenue, external real estate wealth, and municipal redistribution. Hotels, motels, and short-term vacation rentals generate massive revenue streams, with hotel lodging alone bringing in over $6.5 million annually in Transient Lodging Tax (TRT). A significant portion of this lodging tax feeds directly into the City’s General Fund and the Seaside Visitors Bureau.
•	Real Estate & Wealth Inflows: The real estate market operates on capital heavily imported from outside the local job market via remote workers, retirees, and secondary-home/vacation-rental investors. With typical home prices hovering near the upper $400ks, local median household income sits around $47,000.
•	State-Level Tax Dynamics: Oregon lacks a state sales tax, allowing visitors to spend freely at retail shops and outlets along Broadway. Conversely, residents face a state income tax structure of up to 9% for middle-to-higher brackets.
•	Municipal Infrastructure: Facilities like the Seaside Civic & Convention Center act as major economic hubs hosting tournaments and conventions. Municipal funds and lodging taxes are also locked into managing tourism infrastructure debt, such as bonds for the convention center and coastal erosion/storm protection.
•	Summary Financial Flow: Cash is injected by out-of-state/metro tourists and remote equity buyers, captured by local businesses and a 10% Transient Lodging Tax, redistributed into municipal services and marketing, and leaked outward through imported retail goods and external real estate financing costs.
2. Retail Function and Economic Mapping
•	The No-Sales-Tax Advantage: Retail shops, restaurants, and outlets along Broadway benefit immensely from Oregon’s lack of a state sales tax, drawing impulse and leisure spending from Washington and California visitors.
•	High-Velocity Local Circulation: Consumer dollars spent by tourists circulate quickly through small businesses, supporting local commerce before eventually leaking outward for inventory and commercial supply chains.
•	The Cost-of-Living Disconnect: While retail businesses thrive on visitor volume, the local workforce earning a median household income around $47,000 faces upper-$400k property values and up to 9% state income taxes, creating an affordability gap.
3. Retail Logistics and Supply Chain Dynamics
•	Inbound Supply Chains & Regional Corridors: Inventory typically travels via major Willamette Valley or Portland distribution corridors down to the coast, leaving shops vulnerable to highway transit delays or seasonal weather disruptions over the Coast Range.
•	Seasonal Demand Fluctuations: Retailers face dramatic inventory velocity swings—scaling up for summer tourist influxes and major event weekends at the Seaside Civic & Convention Center, then compressing volumes during the off-season.
•	Last-Mile & Downtown Constraints: Dense retail zones around walkable areas like Broadway require tight coordination for commercial deliveries due to limited back-alley loading space and high pedestrian foot traffic.
•	Outbound Financial Leakage: Retail revenue collected from visitors quickly leaves the immediate municipal loop as small businesses pay out external commercial supply chains, corporate wholesale accounts, and out-of-town manufacturing costs.
4. Local Retail Adaptation Strategies
•	Elastic Workforce Scaling: Retailers heavily leverage temporary and seasonal staffing (such as inbound fulfillment and sales associates) to manage massive summer and holiday influxes.
•	Inventory Pre-Positioning: To hedge against transit delays over the Coast Range or winter weather disruptions, local merchants front-load inventory ahead of peak tourism windows and major event weekends.
•	Compact Footprint Efficiency: Given tight commercial real estate limits in high-density zones like Broadway and local outlet centers, businesses optimize small stockrooms for high turnover rather than deep on-site warehousing.




